<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monaco</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Principality of Monaco, Monoikos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Monaco, officially the Principality of Monaco, is a sovereign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> and microstate on the French Riviera, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on three sides and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean Sea</a>. The Genoese built a castle on the rock in the early 1200s, and in 1297 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-grimaldi">Francesco Grimaldi</a> and his men seized it disguised as monks. The Grimaldi family bought Monaco from the Crown of Aragon in 1419, and Honoré II began styling himself Prince in 1612. After periods under France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, Monaco ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menton">Menton</a> and Roquebrune to France, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> was recognised by the Franco-Monégasque Treaty of 1861.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monaco.jpg" alt="Monaco" width="330" height="264" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Drawn by SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Monaco.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Greek historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hecataeus-of-miletus">Hecataeus of Miletus</a> first mentioned the place as Monoikos, a Ligurian town. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> called it Monoikon limen and explained that Monoikos, Greek for 'the solitary one', was another name for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>, who had a temple there, though the name may alter an older Ligurian one. After a grant of land from Emperor Henry VI in 1191, Monaco was refounded in 1215 as a colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. Francesco Grimaldi, known as Malizia, and his men took the fortress on the Rock in 1297 dressed as Franciscan friars; the area was already called Monaco, so the name is a coincidence.</p><p>In 1419 the Grimaldi purchased Monaco from the Crown of Aragon and became its undisputed rulers. Honoré II secured recognition of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1633 and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiii-of-france">Louis XIII of France</a> by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peronne">Péronne</a> in 1641, and in 1642 he was received at Louis XIII's court as a foreign duke and peer. Revolutionary forces captured Monaco in 1793 and the French occupied it until 1814. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> made it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> in 1815, and it passed to French protection in 1860 by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, when French replaced Italian as the official language.</p><p>The princes were absolute rulers until the Monégasque Revolution of 1910 forced the Constitution of 1911. Prince Albert I suspended it during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. A Franco-Monégasque Treaty of July 1918, endorsed in 1919 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>, aligned Monégasque policy with French interests and resolved a succession crisis. A treaty of 2002 provided that Monaco would remain independent even if the Grimaldi line had no heirs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Monaco is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> of 2.08 square kilometres on the French Riviera, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on three sides. Fewer than 10,000 of its residents are Monégasque nationals. French is the official language, with Italian and Monégasque widely spoken, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> is the official religion. It uses the euro and is not a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>. The Casino, built in 1878–9 to designs by Charles Garnier, is the signature of its Belle Époque architecture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Monaco” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Andorra also features in debates about microstates: its admission to the United Nations in 1993, in the same years as San Marino and <b>Monaco</b>, helped show that very small states could take full part in international organisations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/andorra">Andorra · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It is often described as the only member of the old Imperial Diet’s Council of Princes that survives as a sovereign state, and with <b>Monaco</b>, San Marino and Andorra it is one of the European microstates that outlived the consolidation of larger nations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-liechtenstein">Principality of Liechtenstein · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1191 the Emperor Henry VI granted the site to Genoa in return for action against pirates, and the Genoese refounded <b>Monaco</b> as a colony in 1215.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-monaco">Principality of Monaco · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Vatican City was created by the Lateran Treaty of 1929; <b>Monaco</b> has been ruled by the Grimaldi family since 1297, with brief interruptions; and Singapore, which left the Federation of Malaysia in 1965, has been called by The Economist the “world’s only fully functioning city-state”.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In executive or semi-constitutional monarchies, such as Jordan, Morocco, Kuwait, Bahrain, Bhutan, <b>Monaco</b> and Liechtenstein, the monarch keeps substantial powers to appoint and dismiss ministers, veto laws or dissolve the assembly, and the cabinet may answer to both crown and parliament.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>Disguised as a Franciscan friar, the Genoese Guelph François Grimaldi talked his way into the Rock of <b>Monaco</b> and took it.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-8">8 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
